  "worked_solution": "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(12,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-6\\sqrt{3},-6).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
